  "final_message": "I haven't made any changes, because this feature conflicts with ARCH-5.\n\n**The conflict:** ARCH-5 says this service may only call what the architecture catalog declares, which today is only `payments-api`. The inventory Stock API (`stock.inventory.internal`) is a new outbound dependency. It has to be declared in the catalog and approved by Architecture before the service calls it. I found no catalog in this repo and no sign of an approval. I can't grant that approval, so I don't want to ship a call to it.\n\n**What I can do, depending on your answer:**\n1. **Approval exists or is coming.** Tell me where the catalog lives or give me the approval reference. I'll then build the feature with the usual structure:\n   - a `StockGateway` port in `src/core/ports.ts`\n   - an HTTP adapter in `src/adapters/` that calls `GET /v1/stock/{sku}`\n   - a check in `orderService` that throws a new insufficient-stock error\n   - a mapping of that error to 409 in `errorMapper`\n   - `stockGateway` added as an optional dep in `createApp`, so existing tests and callers keep working\n   - unit tests\n2. **Build it ahead of approval.** I can implement everything above but leave the real adapter unwired, using a default that allows all stock. Nothing would call the Stock API until Architecture signs off. The 409 logic and tests would be ready.\n3. **Draft the request.** I can write the catalog entry and approval request text for Architecture.\n\nI also need to know what to do when the Stock API is down or times out. Should the order fail, with a 503 for example, or go through anyway? Which is right depends on how you weigh overselling against lost orders.\n\nWhich option do you want?",
